   "page_count": 2,
   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Moore Kim and Hill Clinic\nPatient: Jacob Jacobs DOB: 03/05/1994 Sex: M MRN: 741775\n\nEncounters:\n 2025-10-21 Malignant melanoma, stage I BP 132/89 Wt 178\n 2024-04-03 Malignant melanoma, stage I BP 145/86 Wt 170\n 2022-09-14 rash BP 147/85 Wt 174\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n • Melanoma cancer, stage I (status post treatment approximately 5.6 years ago) - currently controlled; no medications indicated for this condition.\n • Patient exercises regularly, running 3-4 times per week, which is consistent with his active lifestyle.\n • The patient's social history includes former tobacco use of one pack per day of cigarettes, which he quit in June 2023. The patient has been honest about his tobacco use history.\n • Alcohol consumption is documented at approximately six drinks per week.\n • The patient's weight and blood pressure readings have been within normal ranges during the recent visits.\n \nDetailed Summary:\n\nDr. Michael Best of Moore Kim and Hill Clinic provides the following summary for Jacob Jacobs, a 31-year-old male patient. His medical history includes a diagnosis of malignant melanoma, stage I, which was confirmed on October 4, 2019. This diagnosis has been under control since was completed approximately 5.6 years ago, with no evidence of recurrence or need for further medication.\n\nOn October 21, 2025, Mr. Jacobs presented for a routine follow-up visit regarding his stage I melanoma. His blood pressure was recorded at 132/89, and his weight was 178 pounds. During the visit, Mr. Jacobs reported no new symptoms or concerns related to his melanoma. He continues to maintain a healthy lifestyle, which includes regular exercise, running 3-4 times per week.\n\nMr. Jacobs’ social history reveals that he to smoke cigarettes at a rate of one pack per day, starting on May 22, 2014. He successfully quit smoking on June 5, 2023, and has been honest about his tobacco use history. Additionally, his alcohol consumption is approximately six drinks per week.\n\nOn April 3, 2024, Mr. Jacobs returned for a follow-up visit regarding his stage I melanoma. His blood pressure was 145/86, and his weight was 170 pounds. He again reported no new symptoms or concerns related to his melanoma. His lifestyle choices, including his regular exercise routine and responsible alcohol consumption, are noted to be beneficial for his overall health.\n\nThe last recorded visit before these was on September 14, 2022, when Mr. Jacobs presented with a rash. that time, his blood pressure was 147/85, and his weight was 174 pounds. The rash was evaluated,"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "and no further actions were required as it did not relate to his melanoma.\n\nThroughout the documented visits, Mr. Jacobs has shown no signs of recurrence complications from his melanoma treatment. His family history includes a notable condition; his father had colon cancer, which unfortunately resulted in his death at the age of 55. This history underscores the importance of regular screenings and healthy lifestyle choices for Mr. Jacobs.\n\nThe summary with the ongoing monitoring of Mr. Jacobs’ condition and his commitment to a healthy lifestyle. The patient's active exercise routine and controlled alcohol consumption are positive factors in his ongoing health management. No new medications are required for his melanoma, which remains in a controlled state.\n\nDr. Michael Best continues to provide comprehensive care, ensuring that Mr. Jacobs receives the necessary follow-up and support to maintain his health and well-being.\n"
    }
